  4. The wrapper lacks important calendar-product primitives and a distinctive
     planning surface.
     Calendar sharing/ACL management is explicitly deferred (README2.md:64).
     There is no appointment-schedule/booking-page implementation, and no
     unified task-and-event timeline: CalendarPanel receives events, not
     scheduled tasks (web/src/components/CalendarPanel.tsx:36).

     Also, task priorities and task-to-event scheduling links live only in
     IndexedDB (web/src/data/localStore.ts:36). A user changing browsers/
     devices will see Google events and Tasks, but loses HCB-only scheduling
     context.

  5. Release evidence is not credible yet.
     The full unit suite currently fails: 68/69 tests pass, but all-day
     recurring event creation times out in the full run. It passes alone, so
     this is likely a suite-level timing/flakiness issue, not a proven product
     defect.

     The E2E gate also fails: one of only two browser tests expects an obsolete
     onboarding heading. The other only checks PWA manifest/service-worker
     registration (web/tests/e2e/onboarding.spec.ts:3). There are no browser
     tests for sync, mutations, offline recovery, recurrence, OAuth expiry, or
     managed OAuth.

     The backend is not in CI (.github/workflows/ci.yml:22); locally, its
     typecheck/build and 5 unit tests pass, but they cover config and
     encryption—not PostgreSQL, OAuth callbacks, sessions, proxying, or Google
     failures. The live-Google web procedure exists but has no recorded
     acceptance evidence.

  ## Competitive reality

   Product                 Google Calendar
   Baseline users receive  Shared calendars plus booking pages/availability
                           controls.
   HCB web gap             Sharing is deferred; no booking experience. Google
                           sharing (https://support.google.com/calendar/
                           answer/37082), appointment schedules (https://
                           support.google.com/calendar/answer/10729749)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Product                 Notion Calendar
   Baseline users receive  Web scheduling links, availability blocking,
                           conferencing connections.
   HCB web gap             HCB can find free time but cannot publish/book it.
                           Notion availability (https://www.notion.com/help/
                           availability-blocking-and-time-zones)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Product                 TickTick
   Baseline users receive  Calendar/task integration, collaboration, multi-
                           channel reminders, focused work tools.
   HCB web gap             HCB has a local queue and task-event links, but not
                           a portable unified planner or reliable
                           notifications. TickTick features (https://
                           ticktick.com/features?language=en_US)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Product                 Things 3 / Sorted³
   Baseline users receive  Clear daily planning; Things adds reminders and
                           calendar context, Sorted³ adds a unified timeline
                           and auto-scheduling.
   HCB web gap             HCB’s task and calendar views remain separate; its
                           strongest task-to-calendar feature is hidden in task
                           editing. Things (https://culturedcode.com/things/
                           features/), Sorted³ (https://www.sortedapp.com/how-
                           it-works)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Product                 Apple Calendar
   Baseline users receive  Account-level refresh/push and delegation
                           expectations.
   HCB web gap             HCB’s foreground-only browser model cannot match
                           that trust level. Apple Calendar accounts (https://
                           support.apple.com/en-ie/guide/calendar/icl26669/mac)

  The product has real strengths: local-first cache, incremental Calendar sync
  design, durable queued writes, keyboard-led interaction, Google-native
  recurrence, and a comparatively serious conflict model. But those are not
  enough to outweigh the setup and trust costs for a normal user.

  My recommendation is to settle one product decision before building more
  parity: is HCB a privacy-first BYO-OAuth single-browser tool, or is it a
  hosted, verified, managed-OAuth calendar product? The current repo tries to
  support both, but neither path is complete enough to be the default adoption
  story.
